T*
(Secondary analysis examined parental factors and child quality-of-life outcomes from the) Tj
T*
(baseline cross-sectional assessment. Parental self-reported confidence in ability to) Tj
T*
(promote good oral habits \(self-efficacy\) was the strongest independent predictor of better) Tj
T*
(child oral health-related quality of life \(beta=-0.16, 95% CI -0.24 to -0.09, p<0.001\),) Tj
T*
(indicating children of higher-efficacy parents reported fewer oral-health-related) Tj
T*
(functional limitations and discomfort. Parental knowledge about cavity prevention) Tj
T*
(mechanisms and fluoride benefits showed weaker associations. Household income) Tj
T*
(significantly predicted both parental self-efficacy and child caries outcomes; low-income) Tj
T*
(families showed lower self-efficacy and higher caries rates. Maternal education level) Tj
T*
(correlated positively with parental self-efficacy \(r=0.28, p<0.001\). Among children with) Tj
T*
(caries, quality-of-life impairment included self-consciousness about appearance \(32%\),) Tj
T*
(eating difficulties \(18%\), and school absenteeism \(8%\). Parental self-efficacy mediated) Tj
T*
(the relationship between household socioeconomic factors and child caries risk. These) Tj
T*
(findings highlight that increasing parental confidence in preventive capacity, rather than) Tj
T*
(knowledge alone, may address disparities in childhood oral health outcomes.) Tj
